============================================================================
LZO repository with CPU, GPU, and hybrid execution paths
============================================================================

This tree contains the classic LZO source plus local heterogeneous execution
extensions used for CPU/GPU compression experiments.

Main subprojects:

  lzo_cpu      Native CPU baseline and CLI
  lzo_gpu      OpenCL GPU execution path
  lzo_hybrid   CPU+GPU collaborative path
  tools        benchmark runners and analysis helpers


Build
-----

Linux
~~~~~

CPU baseline:

    cd lzo_cpu
    make gcc

GPU path:

    cd lzo_gpu
    make

Hybrid path:

    cd lzo_hybrid
    make

Typical Linux packages:

  Debian/Ubuntu:
    build-essential opencl-headers ocl-icd-opencl-dev

  Fedora:
    gcc make opencl-headers ocl-icd-devel


Windows
~~~~~~~

Supported Windows build target is MSYS2 / MinGW-w64.

This is the supported path because the current codebase uses Makefiles and
pthread-based CPU/hybrid paths. MSYS2 gives a practical Windows environment
without requiring a full MSVC-native port.

Install MSYS2 packages first:

    pacman -S --needed mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-make mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-winpthreads

You can then build against CUDA-installed OpenCL headers/import libraries:

    cd lzo_gpu
    make OS=Windows_NT CC=gcc \
      OPENCL_INCLUDE_DIR="/c/Program Files/NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit/CUDA/v13.1/include" \
      OPENCL_LIB_DIR="/c/Program Files/NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit/CUDA/v13.1/lib/x64"

    cd ../lzo_hybrid
    make OS=Windows_NT CC=gcc \
      OPENCL_INCLUDE_DIR="/c/Program Files/NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit/CUDA/v13.1/include" \
      OPENCL_LIB_DIR="/c/Program Files/NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit/CUDA/v13.1/lib/x64"

    cd ../lzo_cpu
    make win32-mingw

Notes:

  - NVIDIA CUDA on Windows includes OpenCL headers and import libraries.
  - The OpenCL runtime DLL is provided by the installed GPU driver.
  - If your OpenCL import library name differs, override OPENCL_LIB_NAME.
  - Current Windows support focuses on standalone and benchmark modes.
  - Daemon/client mode in lzo_gpu remains Linux-only today.


Benchmarking
------------

Important runners:

  tools/bench_lzo.py      CPU / GPU / hybrid benchmark driver
  tools/README.md         tooling overview

Example:

    python3 tools/bench_lzo.py --samples-dir /root/samples_subset --hybrid-only --bench-seconds 1


Current status
--------------

  - lzo_gpu is currently the default strongest verified engine in the LZO family.
  - lzo_hybrid has received recent scheduler, gather/scatter, and bench-path fixes.
  - lzo_cpu remains the preferred CPU baseline on the current Intel platform.
